PRESERVATION_OVERRIDES['ASSIGN-W11-Q07']={'originalPrompt':'During Weight Update of training an MLP Regression model using backpropagation, what equation governs updating weight ωᵢⱼ?','originalOptions':[{'sourceLabel':'A','text':'wᵢⱼ(new) = wᵢⱼ(old) + η ∂Loss/∂wᵢⱼ(old)'},{'sourceLabel':'B','text':'wᵢⱼ(new) = wᵢⱼ(old) − ∂Loss/∂wᵢⱼ(old)'},{'sourceLabel':'C','text':'wᵢⱼ(new) = ∂Loss/∂wᵢⱼ(old)'},{'sourceLabel':'D','text':'wᵢⱼ(new) = wᵢⱼ(old) − η ∂Loss/∂wᵢⱼ(old)'}],'_inspectedAt':NOW()}
qs=[]
qs.append(Q(11,1,1,'Ideal battery runtime and energy budget','A robot continuously draws 10 W for motors, 5 W for computing and 5 W for sensors. Its battery is modeled as a fully usable 12 V × 2.5 Ah energy store, with no additional losses. What are the stored energy and ideal runtime?', ['30 Wh and 90 min','30 Wh and 180 min','2.5 Wh and 7.5 min','30 Wh and 1.5 min'],['Energy is 12×2.5=30 Wh; total load is 20 W, giving 1.5 h or 90 min.','180 min neglects the combined 10 W computing and sensor load.','Ampere-hours are charge capacity, not watt-hours; the voltage factor was omitted.','1.5 is the runtime in hours, not minutes.'],[C(50,'Week 11 item 1, battery/load prompt and energy/runtime working','Motor 10 W, compute 5 W, sensors 5 W; 12 V, 2.5 Ah; energy 30 Wh and constant-load runtime 1.5 h = 90 min.','equation')],'Convert battery charge capacity to energy, add simultaneous loads, then divide energy by power. This is an ideal estimate, not a measured battery-life promise.',assumptions=['Constant stated voltage and powers; full nominal energy usable; ignore conversion losses, reserves and discharge-rate effects.'],steps=['P=10+5+5=20 W.','E=12×2.5=30 Wh.','t=30/20=1.5 h=90 min.'],formulas=[F('E=V C; t=E/P',{'E':('usable battery energy','Wh'),'V':('constant battery voltage','V'),'C':('usable charge capacity','Ah'),'P':('total continuous load power','W'),'t':('runtime','h')},['Constant loads and voltage; all stated energy available; no extra losses.'])],qtype='numerical',angles=['power_sum','Ah_vs_Wh','runtime_units','ideal_vs_actual']))
qs.append(Q(11,2,2,'Pneumatic precision limits','Which pair of physical effects does Week 11 item 2 identify as making accurate pneumatic actuator positioning difficult?', ['Compressibility of the working air and seal friction.','Near-incompressibility of the working air and seal friction.','Compressibility of the working air and frictionless seals.','Near-incompressibility of the working air and frictionless seals.'],['The source identifies both air compressibility and high seal friction as control difficulties.','This reverses the stated air property while retaining the friction issue.','This removes the stated friction issue rather than identifying both causes.','Neither property matches the source’s pair of physical limitations.'],[C(50,'Week 11 item 2, pneumatic-control prompt, four choices and printed answer','The identified limitations are compressibility of air and high seal friction.')],'A compressible working medium and friction both complicate the relationship between control input and motion. This explains a precision challenge, not an assertion that pneumatic control is impossible.',angles=['physical_properties','compressibility_vs_incompressibility','friction']))
qs.append(Q(11,3,3,'ADC top bin versus endpoint estimate','Use an ideal 10-bit ADC with 5 V reference and the explicit quantizer k=floor(1024 Vᵢₙ/5) for 0≤Vᵢₙ<5 V. A display reconstructs voltage as V̂=5k/1023. If k=1023, which pair gives the display value and the possible input interval?', ['V̂=5 V; 4.9951171875 V ≤ Vᵢₙ < 5 V.','V̂=5 V; Vᵢₙ must equal exactly 5 V.','V̂=3.3 V; 3.29677734375 V ≤ Vᵢₙ < 3.3 V.','V̂=2.5 V; 2.5 V ≤ Vᵢₙ < 2.5048828125 V.'],['The endpoint reconstruction is 5 V, but floor quantization maps a finite top bin to code 1023.','This confuses the displayed endpoint estimate with a uniquely determined input; exactly 5 V is outside the stated domain.','This substitutes a 3.3 V reference despite the explicitly fixed 5 V model.','This treats the maximum code as a midpoint code and therefore selects the wrong interval.'],[C(51,'Week 11 item 3, maximum ADC code and 0–5 V explanation','The original gives code 1023 and keys 5 V; its explanation supplies a 0–5 V, 0–1023 convention without distinguishing code-bin uncertainty.','statement','conflict')],'A digital code identifies a quantization bin, not one exact analog voltage. The explicit model makes the endpoint display convention and the top input interval separately testable.',assumptions=['The quantizer and reconstruction equations are supplied modeling conventions, not a universal Arduino specification; no saturation or noise; input below 5 V.'],steps=['V̂=5×1023/1023=5 V.','floor(1024 Vᵢₙ/5)=1023 requires 1023≤1024 Vᵢₙ/5<1024.','Multiply by 5/1024: 5115/1024≤Vᵢₙ<5, giving the stated interval.'],formulas=[F('k=floor(1024 Vᵢₙ/Vᵣₑf); V̂=k Vᵣₑf/1023',{'k':('10-bit output code','integer code'),'Vᵢₙ':('analog input','V'),'Vᵣₑf':('fixed reference, 5','V'),'V̂':('endpoint-reconstructed display value','V')},['Ideal supplied floor quantizer; 0≤Vᵢₙ<Vᵣₑf; 1024 codes; no clipping/noise.'])],qtype='numerical',difficulty='medium',conflict={'issue':'The original stem does not identify resolution or reference and treats maximum code as an exact input voltage.','resolution':'Supply the 10-bit/5 V quantizer and endpoint-display conventions explicitly; distinguish the input bin from reconstruction.','method':'Solve the floor inequality and separately evaluate the reconstruction formula.'},angles=['reference_and_resolution','code_bin','endpoint_reconstruction','source_qualification']))
qs.append(Q(11,4,4,'Encoder pulse-count convention','An encoder generates 600 cycles per shaft revolution. Counter A records one count per cycle; counter B records four counts per cycle. Each counter independently reports 1800 counts after a one-direction motion with no missed counts. What rotation amounts do the two readings represent, respectively?', ['3 revolutions and 0.75 revolution.','3 revolutions and 12 revolutions.','0.75 revolution and 3 revolutions.','3 revolutions and 3 revolutions.'],['A uses 600 counts/revolution; B uses 2400 counts/revolution, so 1800/600=3 and 1800/2400=0.75.','The fourfold count density must divide the inferred rotations, not multiply them.','This assigns the four-count mode to A and one-count mode to B.','This ignores the explicitly different counting conventions.'],[C(51,'Week 11 item 4, numeric encoder prompt and count/PPR calculation','600 PPR and 1800 pulses produce 3 rotations when counted pulses use the stated PPR convention.','equation')],'The denominator must be counts per revolution for the active decoder mode, not an unqualified encoder label. The four-count convention is explicitly supplied as a comparison.',assumptions=['The two readings refer to separate motions, not simultaneous readings of the same motion; shaft rotates only one direction; no missed counts.'],steps=['A: 600 cycles/revolution × 1 count/cycle =600 counts/revolution.','B: 600×4=2400 counts/revolution.','Divide 1800 counts by each corresponding count density.'],formulas=[F('R=N/(m P)',{'R':('shaft rotation amount','revolutions'),'N':('recorded count','counts'),'m':('decoder multiplicity','counts/cycle'),'P':('encoder cycle density','cycles/revolution')},['Unidirectional motion; no missed counts; use the stipulated decoder mode.'])],qtype='numerical',difficulty='medium',angles=['count_convention','rotation_fraction','quadrature_multiplier']))
qs.append(Q(11,5,5,'Geared stall torque as a stipulated model','For this calculation only, use the effective torque-transfer model Tₒᵤₜ=η G Tₘ even at stall, with η=0.85, motor-to-load reduction G=20 and motor stall torque Tₘ=0.25 N·m. The motor’s 3000 rpm no-load speed is not its stall speed. What output torque does this model predict?', ['4.25 N·m','5.00 N·m','0.010625 N·m','5.88235294 N·m'],['0.85×20×0.25=4.25 N·m in the stipulated effective-transfer model.','5 N·m is the ideal gearing result before applying the 0.85 factor.','This divides by the reduction ratio rather than multiplying motor torque by it.','This divides by 0.85, incorrectly making the loss factor increase delivered torque.'],[C(51,'Week 11 item 5, motor, gearbox and efficiency input values','G=20 motor:load; motor stall torque 0.25 N·m; no-load speed 3000 rpm; efficiency 85%.','equation'),C(52,'Week 11 item 5 continuation, numeric answer and torque multiplication','The solution applies T_out=0.25×20×0.85=4.25 N·m at stall.','equation','conflict')],'The supplied effective torque multiplier gives 4.25 N·m. At zero rotational speed, a power-efficiency ratio is not determined by this arithmetic, so this is not a measured stall rating or continuous safe torque.',assumptions=['η is stipulated as an effective torque-transfer factor for this calculation; do not infer a physical power-efficiency ratio at zero speed or a safe operating envelope.'],steps=['Ideal geared torque: G Tₘ=20×0.25=5 N·m.','Apply stipulated transfer factor: 0.85×5=4.25 N·m.','Stall means zero speed; the separate no-load speed does not enter this multiplication.'],formulas=[F('Tₒᵤₜ=η G Tₘ',{'Tₒᵤₜ':('model output torque','N·m'),'Tₘ':('motor torque','N·m'),'G':('motor-to-load reduction ratio','dimensionless'),'η':('stipulated effective torque-transfer factor','dimensionless')},['The factor is assumed applicable at the stated operating point for this exercise only.'])],qtype='numerical',conflict={'issue':'The source applies an 85% gearbox efficiency multiplier at stall without distinguishing a torque-transfer approximation from a zero-speed power-efficiency ratio.','resolution':'Treat 0.85 explicitly as an effective torque-transfer factor, not a measured stall power efficiency.','method':'Apply the supplied scalar model and state its limitation.'},angles=['gear_ratio','loss_factor','stall_vs_no_load','model_scope']))
qs.append(Q(11,6,6,'Stable motor operating point','Assume motor torque decreases continuously with positive shaft speed, opposing load torque is constant, and the curves meet at ω*. With positive inertia and unchanged curves, the motor is momentarily at a speed just below ω*. What follows?', ['Motor torque exceeds load torque, so speed initially increases toward ω*.','Motor torque is below load torque, so speed initially decreases away from ω*.','Motor and load torque remain equal, so the lower speed is another equilibrium.','Motor torque exceeds load torque, so speed initially decreases toward zero.'],['A decreasing motor curve has higher torque below its intersection with the constant load, leaving positive accelerating torque.','This reverses the inequality implied by the decreasing motor curve.','The stipulated decrease and constant load give an isolated crossing, not equality at every nearby speed.','This gets the torque ordering right but reverses the sign of angular acceleration.'],[C(52,'Week 11 item 6, below-equilibrium prompt and highlighted torque comparison','Below the source’s operating point, motor torque exceeds load torque and the system accelerates toward it.')],'Speed is pulled toward this stable crossing because the motor has a torque surplus below it. The curve assumptions matter; “below equilibrium” alone does not determine stability for every possible load curve.',assumptions=['Strictly decreasing motor torque locally; constant opposing load; positive inertia; no changed voltage, load or controller mode.'],formulas=[F('J dω/dt=Tₘ(ω)−Tₗ',{'J':('rotational inertia','kg·m²'),'ω':('shaft angular velocity','rad/s'),'t':('time','s'),'Tₘ':('motor torque','N·m'),'Tₗ':('opposing load torque','N·m')},['Positive J; stated local torque curves; both torques referred to the same shaft.'])],angles=['torque_inequality','acceleration_sign','stability_assumptions']))
qs.append(Q(11,7,7,'Gradient-descent sign and scale','Backpropagation gives ∂L/∂w=−3 at the current weight w=0.4. For one plain gradient-descent step with learning rate η=0.02 and no momentum, regularization or adaptive scaling, what new weight is obtained?', ['0.46','0.34','3.40','−0.06'],['w_new=0.4−0.02×(−3)=0.46; a negative gradient increases the weight.','This adds η times the gradient instead of subtracting it.','This omits the learning-rate factor and uses 0.4−(−3).','This keeps only the scaled gradient and discards the old weight.'],[C(52,'Week 11 item 7, update prompt and choices A/B','The prompt asks the weight-update equation; A adds the scaled gradient and B subtracts an unscaled gradient.','equation'),C(53,'Week 11 item 7 continuation, choices C/D and printed key','Choice D is w_new=w_old−η ∂Loss/∂w_old.','equation')],'Backpropagation computes the gradient; the explicitly chosen optimizer uses that gradient to update the weight. Subtracting a negative gradient moves the weight upward.',assumptions=['One scalar update; supplied gradient evaluated at the old weight; all toy weight/loss quantities dimensionless; no actual training run claimed.'],steps=['Scaled gradient: η∂L/∂w=0.02×(−3)=−0.06.','Subtract it from the old value: 0.4−(−0.06)=0.46.'],formulas=[F('w_new=w_old−η ∂L/∂w_old',{'w_new':('updated weight','dimensionless'),'w_old':('current weight','dimensionless'),'η':('learning rate','dimensionless'),'L':('toy loss','dimensionless')},['Plain gradient descent; gradient evaluated at old parameters; no additional optimizer terms.'])],qtype='numerical',angles=['gradient_sign','learning_rate','gradient_vs_optimizer']))
qs.append(Q(11,8,8,'SVM decision score versus geometric distance','A one-dimensional classifier uses f(x)=2.5x−7.5, with x measured in mm/s; positive f means Normal (+1), negative f means Faulty (−1). Define signed geometric distance as displacement along increasing x from the boundary f(x)=0. At x=3.2 mm/s, which tuple (class, decision score, signed geometric distance) is correct?', ['(Normal, 0.5, +0.2 mm/s)','(Normal, 0.5, +0.5 mm/s)','(Faulty, −0.5, −0.2 mm/s)','(Normal, 0.2, +0.5 mm/s)'],['The boundary is x=3; f(3.2)=0.5>0, and the displacement is 3.2−3=+0.2 mm/s.','This mistakes the unnormalized decision score for a geometric distance.','Both the score sign and the position relative to the boundary are reversed.','This swaps the numerical roles of score and geometric distance.'],[C(53,'Week 11 item 8, classifier, measurement, answer pairs and calculation','f(x)=2.5x−7.5, x=3.2 mm/s; Normal is +1. Highlighted option A says Normal, distance=0.5; working computes f(3.2)=0.5.','equation','conflict')],'The class depends on the sign of the score. Geometric distance depends on the location of the boundary in feature units. The source’s 0.5 calculation is a decision score, not the requested feature-space distance.',assumptions=['One unscaled feature axis; score is dimensionless, so slope 2.5 has units (mm/s)⁻¹; positive distance is toward increasing x; x is not on the boundary.'],steps=['Solve 2.5x₀−7.5=0, obtaining x₀=3 mm/s.','f(3.2)=8−7.5=0.5, hence Normal.','Signed displacement d=x−x₀=0.2 mm/s; equivalently f/|2.5|=0.2 mm/s.'],formulas=[F('f(x)=a x+b; x₀=−b/a; d=x−x₀=f(x)/|a| for a>0',{'f':('decision score','dimensionless'),'a':('positive slope, 2.5','(mm/s)⁻¹'),'b':('intercept, −7.5','dimensionless'),'x':('RMS vibration feature','mm/s'),'x₀':('decision-boundary feature value','mm/s'),'d':('signed geometric distance toward increasing x','mm/s')},['One-dimensional unscaled feature; a>0; Euclidean feature-space displacement.'])],qtype='numerical',difficulty='medium',conflict={'issue':'The original keys Normal with geometric distance 0.5, but 0.5 is the raw decision score; none of the original options gives distance 0.2.','resolution':'Separate score 0.5 from signed geometric distance +0.2 mm/s while retaining the source class labels.','method':'Find the boundary algebraically and subtract its feature coordinate from 3.2.'},angles=['class_sign','decision_score','geometric_distance','source_error']))
qs.append(Q(11,9,9,'Training-set standardization scope','A pipeline fits the per-feature transformation z=(x−μ_train)/σ_train on training data, then uses those same training statistics on new inputs. Assume a nonconstant feature and population-standard-deviation normalization. Which statement accurately describes this operation?', ['The transformed training feature has mean zero and variance one; a new input batch need not have either statistic.','Every new input batch has mean zero and variance one, even though no statistics are refitted.','It maps every training value into [0,1] and preserves that interval for every new input.','It constrains the predicted output torque to be nonnegative while leaving input scale unchanged.'],['Centering and scaling using fitted statistics standardizes that training feature, not arbitrary later distributions.','A new batch can have a different mean and spread; reusing training statistics does not enforce its sample moments.','This confuses standardization with min–max scaling and adds an unwarranted bounded-range claim.','The operation acts on input features; it does not impose a sign constraint on model outputs.'],[C(53,'Week 11 item 9, StandardScaler-before-MLPRegressor prompt','The prompt asks why input StandardScaler precedes inverse-dynamics MLPRegressor.'),C(54,'Week 11 item 9 continuation, options and printed answer','The source identifies zero-mean, unit-variance input normalization.')],'Use the training data to establish a common feature scale. The nonconstant-feature and fitted-training-data qualifications prevent an overbroad “all data always has unit variance” claim.',assumptions=['Centering and scaling enabled; finite nonconstant training feature; population variance; same fitted transformation reused without refitting.'],formulas=[F('z=(x−μ_train)/σ_train',{'z':('standardized input feature','dimensionless'),'x':('original feature value','feature units'),'μ_train':('training-feature mean','feature units'),'σ_train':('positive training-feature population standard deviation','feature units')},['σ_train>0; statistics fitted on training data only; same transform applied to new data.'])],angles=['training_vs_test','standardization_vs_minmax','nonconstant_scope','inputs_vs_outputs']))
qs.append(Q(11,10,10,'Inverse-dynamics toy prediction and error','A supplied joint-torque predictor is τ̂=2θ+0.5θ̇+1.5θ̈+0.8, with coefficients carrying the units needed for τ̂ in N·m when θ is in rad, θ̇ in rad/s and θ̈ in rad/s². At (θ,θ̇,θ̈)=(0.5,2,3), measured torque is 8.0 N·m. What are τ̂ and absolute prediction error?', ['7.3 N·m and 0.7 N·m','6.5 N·m and 1.5 N·m','7.3 N·m and −0.7 N·m','8.8 N·m and 0.8 N·m'],['The four terms sum to 1+1+4.5+0.8=7.3; |8−7.3|=0.7.','This omits the intercept 0.8 from the prediction.','An absolute error cannot be negative; −0.7 is prediction minus measurement.','This substitutes 1.5 for the velocity coefficient 0.5, adding an extra 2 N·m, so it does not evaluate the stated model.'],[C(54,'Week 11 item 10, predictor equation, inputs, measurement and working','τhat=2θ+0.5θdot+1.5θddot+0.8; inputs .5,2,3; measured 8 N·m; predicted 7.3 N·m and absolute error .7 N·m.','equation')],'Evaluate every term, including the intercept, before comparing with the measurement. This evaluates a supplied toy model; it does not show a trained model’s accuracy on other motions.',assumptions=['No rounding until the stated decimal results; the expression is supplied rather than trained here; one joint and one measurement.'],steps=['Position term: 2×0.5=1 N·m.','Velocity term: 0.5×2=1 N·m; acceleration term: 1.5×3=4.5 N·m.','Add intercept: 1+1+4.5+0.8=7.3 N·m.','Absolute error: |8.0−7.3|=0.7 N·m.'],formulas=[F('τ̂=aθ+bθ̇+cθ̈+d; e_abs=|τ_measured−τ̂|',{'τ̂':('predicted torque','N·m'),'θ':('joint angle','rad'),'θ̇':('joint velocity','rad/s'),'θ̈':('joint acceleration','rad/s²'),'a':('position coefficient, 2','N·m/rad'),'b':('velocity coefficient, 0.5','N·m·s/rad'),'c':('acceleration coefficient, 1.5','N·m·s²/rad'),'d':('intercept, 0.8','N·m'),'τ_measured':('measured torque, 8','N·m'),'e_abs':('absolute prediction error','N·m')},['Evaluate the given scalar predictor with the supplied units and inputs.'])],qtype='numerical',angles=['all_predictor_terms','intercept','absolute_vs_signed_error','units']))
